# Go Gateway Web UI

Build a Go HTTP gateway that serves a full multi-page web UI (home, installer/setup, admin dashboard, docs) alongside REST and SSE API endpoints — all from a single binary with zero external files.

## When to use

- User wants a "web UI for my Go gateway" / "admin dashboard" / "installer page" / "docs page"
- User wants a single-binary Go app that serves both API and HTML
- User wants inline HTML/CSS/JS (no npm, no build step, no external files)
- User wants SSE streaming from a Go server
- User wants a dark-theme responsive UI embedded in Go handlers

## Architecture choice: single-const SPA vs. multi-page

Once the UI passes ~3 views (dashboard, models, settings…) collapse them into ONE inline-template `page` const served by ONE `Handler()` and let the JS do the routing. The multi-page `homePage`/`installerPage`/`adminPage` pattern shown below is the starting shape; the mature shape is one `const page = `<!doctype html>...`` (1.5k–2k lines of HTML/CSS/JS) plus a SPA state-machine in the embedded `<script>`. See `references/omnistream-gateway.md` for the canonical running implementation (live endpoint inventory, the 503 routing_error gotcha, production readiness gaps, and the Hermes display-mask workaround), and `templates/spa-handler.go` for a copy-and-edit starter that drops into any `internal/web/`. Probe helper: `scripts/probe-gateway.py`.

## Architecture

### Multi-mux composition

The root mux delegates to sub-muxes by path prefix:

```go
apiMux := gateways.NewMux(registry, router, authStore, streamHandler)
webMux := web.Handler()

mux := http.NewServeMux()
mux.Handle("/v1/", apiMux)
mux.Handle("/stream", apiMux)
mux.Handle("/health", apiMux)
mux.Handle("/", webMux)  // web UI catches everything else
```

The web handler mux serves:
- `/` — landing/hero page
- `/install` — provider configuration form
- `/admin` — gateway administration dashboard
- `/docs` — API reference with tabbed SDK examples

### Port configuration

```go
port := "8800"
if p := os.Getenv("PORT"); p != "" {
    port = p
}
addr := fmt.Sprintf(":%s", port)
```

## Inline HTML design system

All HTML/CSS/JS is embedded as Go string literals in handler functions. Key patterns:

### CSS custom properties (dark theme)

```css
:root {
    --bg0: #0a0b0f; --bg1: #10121a; --bg2: #161a26;
    --surface: #1c2035; --line: rgba(255,255,255,0.08);
    --text: #f0f2ff; --muted: #9aa0cc; --dim: #6b7199;
    --primary: #7c6fff; --primary-glow: rgba(124,111,255,0.3);
    --accent: #00e5ff; --accent-glow: rgba(0,229,255,0.2);
    --green: #00f5a0; --amber: #ffb347; --red: #ff5f6d;
    --radius: 14px; --radius-sm: 8px;
}
```

### Background effect

```css
body {
    background: radial-gradient(ellipse at 50% -20%, #1a1c3a 0%, var(--bg0) 50%);
}
body::before {
    content: ''; position: fixed; inset: 0;
    background:
        radial-gradient(circle at 20% 50%, rgba(124,111,255,0.15) 0%, transparent 50%),
        radial-gradient(circle at 80% 20%, rgba(0,229,255,0.08) 0%, transparent 50%);
    pointer-events: none; z-index: -1;
}
```

### Glassmorphism topbar

```css
.topbar {
    display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between;
    padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 32px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    backdrop-filter: blur(10px);
    background: rgba(10,11,15,0.6);
    position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 100;
}
```

### Card pattern

```css
.card {
    background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius); padding: 24px;
    transition: transform 0.2s, box-shadow 0.2s;
}
.card:hover {
    transform: translateY(-2px);
    box-shadow: 0 8px 24px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);
}
```

### Button variants

```css
