.btn.primary { background: var(--primary); color: #fff; box-shadow: 0 0 20px var(--primary-glow); }
.btn.secondary { background: transparent; border: 1px solid var(--line2); color: var(--text); }
.btn.danger { background: var(--red); color: #fff; box-shadow: 0 0 20px var(--red-glow); }
```

### Table pattern

```css
.table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; }
.table th { text-align: left; padding: 12px 16px; color: var(--dim); font-size: 0.75rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.08em; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
.table td { padding: 12px 16px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
```

### Status badges

```css
.badge { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 6px; padding: 4px 12px; border-radius: 20px; font-size: 0.8rem; font-weight: 600; }
.badge.green { background: rgba(0,245,160,0.15); color: var(--green); }
.badge.amber { background: rgba(255,179,71,0.15); color: var(--amber); }
.badge.red { background: rgba(255,95,109,0.15); color: var(--red); }
```

### Stat card grid

```css
.stats { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(160px, 1fr)); gap: 16px; margin-bottom: 32px; }
.stat-card { background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius); padding: 20px; text-align: center; }
.stat-card .value { font-size: 2rem; font-weight: 800; color: var(--primary); }
.stat-card .label { font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--dim); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.08em; }
```

## SSE streaming (critical pitfall)

**Root cause of silent SSE hang:** The Go HTTP handler must call `WriteHeader(http.StatusOK)` AND `Flush()` before the SSE loop starts. Without `WriteHeader`, the server never commits the response. Without `Flush`, the client never sees the first byte.

```go
func StreamHandler(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
    flusher, ok := w.(http.Flusher)
    if !ok {
        http.Error(w, "streaming not supported", http.StatusInternalServerError)
        return
    }
    w.Header().Set("Content-Type", "text/event-stream")
    w.Header().Set("Cache-Control", "no-cache")
    w.Header().Set("Connection", "keep-alive")
    w.WriteHeader(http.StatusOK)  // REQUIRED
    flusher.Flush()               // REQUIRED

    for {
        data := <-eventCh
        fmt.Fprintf(w, "data: %s\n\n", data)
        flusher.Flush()
    }
}
```

**Testing SSE:** Use raw TCP connections with deadlines, not `io.ReadAll(response.Body)` (which blocks forever on an open stream).

## Page patterns

### Home page
- Hero section with title + version/protocol/provider/route stat badges
- Feature cards (3-column grid)
- Quick Start section with copyable code snippet
- CTA button to Setup

### Setup/Install page
- Provider config form: type dropdown (Ollama/OpenAI/Anthropic/Custom), base URL, model ID, route name, secret env field
- Progress tracker (visual step indicators)
- Test prompt textarea with "Test Connection" button

### Admin page
- Gateway status badges (Online, Protocol, Streaming)
- Stat cards (Gateway, Protocol, Providers, Routes)
- Provider Health table (name, status, protocol, last check)
- Available Models list
- Recent Events feed
- API Playground (auth key, route, prompt inputs + Send/Clear)

### Docs page
- Tabbed SDK switcher (OpenAI SDK, Python, Node.js, cURL)
- Copyable code examples for chat completions, streaming, model listing
- Each tab shows the same API call in different SDKs

## Pitfalls

1. **SSE stream hangs silently** — always call `WriteHeader(200)` + `Flush()` before the SSE loop. Without `WriteHeader`, the response is never committed. Without `Flush`, the client never sees the first byte.
2. **Multi-mux ordering** — put the catch-all web handler (`/`) LAST in the root mux, after API prefixes (`/v1/`, `/stream`, `/health`). Otherwise API routes get swallowed by the web handler.
3. **Backtick collision: Go raw strings vs JS template literals** — Go backtick-delimited raw string literals (`const s = \`...\``) CANNOT contain backticks. But JavaScript template literals also use backticks. When embedding a large SPA with JS template literals inside a Go `const` string, you MUST convert all JS template literals to string concatenation. Example:
   ```go
   // WRONG — backtick inside Go raw string literal
   const html = `<script>
   const name = \`Hello \${user}\`;  // SYNTAX ERROR
   </script>`

   // RIGHT — use string concatenation instead
   const html = `<script>
   const name = "Hello " + user;  // OK
   </script>`
   ```
   For multi-line template literals, convert to `"..." +` concatenation. For template expressions like `` `Hello ${user}` `` use `"Hello " + user + ""`. For complex expressions like `` `data: ${JSON.stringify(x)}` `` use `"data: " + JSON.stringify(x)`.
4. **`fmt.Fprintf` format verb collision with SVG data URIs** — SVG data URIs contain `%3C`, `%3E`, `%20`, `%23` etc. which Go's `fmt.Fprintf` interprets as format verbs (`%3C` → unknown verb C, compile error). NEVER use `fmt.Fprintf` with a document that contains SVG data URIs. Instead, use `strings.Replace` with custom placeholders:
   ```go
   // WRONG — SVG data URI has %3C, %3E etc.
   fmt.Fprintf(w, document, title)  // compile error: unknown verb C

   // RIGHT — use strings.Replace with {PLACEHOLDER} tokens
   s := strings.Replace(document, "{TITLE}", title, 1)
   s = strings.Replace(s, "{BODY}", body, 1)
   w.Write([]byte(s))
   ```
   The document constant uses `{TITLE}` and `{BODY}` as placeholders instead of `%s`.
5. **Font loading** — Google Fonts via `<link>` works but adds a render-blocking request. Prefer `font-display: swap` in the CSS (Google Fonts does this by default).
6. **Copy button** — `navigator.clipboard.writeText()` requires HTTPS or localhost. On HTTP-only deployments, fall back to `document.execCommand('copy')` or a textarea-select pattern.
7. **Test updates** — when redesigning UI text, update `web_test.go` strings to match new headings. Tests check for exact substring matches. When converting from multi-page to SPA, the test must change from checking multiple paths (`/install`, `/admin`) to checking the single `/` route and adding method-not-allowed tests.
8. **Port conflicts** — check `lsof -i :PORT` before starting. Kill stale processes with `pkill -f modelport` or by PID.
9. **`patch` tool `replace_all=true` footgun with version tokens** — replacing an old version string that appears in two structurally different contexts (e.g. a `<span class="topbar-version">v0.3</span>` and a separate `<span class="sidebar-version">v0.3</span>`) WILL splice the two contexts together, corrupting markup. When bumping a version, paste 2-3 lines of surrounding context to make the `old_string` unique, OR rewrite the whole region with `write_file`. Never use `replace_all=true` for version-string bumps unless you've verified there's exactly one match in the file.
10. **Single-const template convention** — once the UI matures, collapse all per-page `homePage`/`installerPage`/`adminPage` functions into ONE `page` string const served by one `Handler()`. The frontend becomes a SPA with a `STATE.view` field and `render()` switch. Saves embedding overhead, lets the command palette jump between views, and removes per-route test cases. Update `web_test.go` to a single `TestHandlerServesSPA` + a `TestHandlerRejectsNonGET`.
11. **Token compatibility aliases when upgrading CSS** — when introducing a new design-token system (e.g. `--brand-500` / `--bg-2` ramps), DON'T rename every old `var(--brand)` reference in the JS-rendered HTML. Add a `:root` alias block that maps old names onto new ones (`--brand: var(--brand-600); --bg-panel: var(--bg-2);`). The JS class contracts stay valid and your design upgrade is a single CSS swap, not a full template rewrite.
12. **Verification ladder for inline-template UI changes** — test caching hides broken templates. After editing `page` constants: (a) `go clean -testcache && make test` — proves the literal still compiles and the route still serves; (b) live HTTP smoke (`curl http://localhost:PORT/`) and confirm the new tokens are present in the body; (c) browser screenshot of the actual render — proves the design upgrade landed and not just a syntax pass. Skipping any one of these lets stale evidence mask real regressions.
13. **`escHtml` is mandatory for any user-controlled string rendered into innerHTML in the SPA** — every `.map() → innerHTML +=` must wrap with `escHtml(x)` or content containing `<` / `>` / `"` poisons the DOM (model IDs, base URLs, command-palette items are all untrusted strings). `escHtml` is just `document.createElement('div').textContent = s; return d.innerHTML;` — cheap, mandatory, never skip it.
14. **go:embed and the single-page template `go:embed *.go` pattern** — when `Handler()` references the page via `template.Must(template.New("web").Parse(page))` you do NOT need `go:embed` at all (Go source files are already on disk at build time). If you later want to keep the template in a sibling `.tmpl` file, add `//go:embed *.tmpl` and `template.ParseFS(WebFS, "page.tmpl")`. Reserve `go:embed` for HTML/CSS/JS asset files that don't have a `.go` counterpart.
15. **Hash routing in inline-template SPAs is a footgun — pick ONE source of truth for STATE.view** — when a SPA's sidebar nav does `onclick="window.location.hash='models'"` but the SPA's render function only reads `STATE.view` (with no `window.addEventListener('hashchange', ...)`), the URL changes but the view does NOT. Symptom: clicking sidebar items visibly does nothing (browser_snapshot shows the same content before/after); only direct `STATE.view = 'x'` or programmatic navigation via `switchView('x')` works. Two correct patterns — pick one and document it:
    - **(a) Hash-driven** — sidebar nav writes `location.hash`; add `window.addEventListener('hashchange', () => switchView(location.hash.slice(1) || 'dashboard'))`; `switchView` writes back to `location.hash` for forward/back buttons. One listener, both sources stay in sync.
    - **(b) STATE-driven** — sidebar nav calls `onclick="switchView('models')"` directly; `switchView` updates `STATE.view` and `render()`. Optionally mirror to `history.pushState({}, '', '#' + view)` so the URL still reflects state and refresh works. No `hashchange` listener needed.
    Mixing the two (hash writes but no listener) is the bug. Verified against Omnistream v4 — sidebar onclick changed `window.location.hash` to `#models` but the SPA kept rendering Playground because no `hashchange` listener existed.
16. **Headless browser form fill: `element.value = 'x'` does NOT work for controlled inputs in inline-template SPAs** — the SPA often re-renders on every keystroke via `STATE.X` state-binding, and setting `.value` directly bypasses the input event contract. The reliable path:
    ```js
    el.focus();
    document.execCommand('insertText', false, 'your text here');
    ```
    `execCommand('insertText')` dispatches the `input` event the framework is listening for, so STATE.X gets the new value. Verified on Omnistream Playground system-prompt + message fields — `.value =` returned empty after re-render, `execCommand('insertText')` stuck. See `references/omnistream-gateway.md` § "Interactive walkthrough playbook" for the full pattern (form fill → click handler discovery → Send → read assistant response from STATE.chat).
17. **`browser_vision` and `vision_analyze` hallucinate UI text on screenshots** — when asked to "describe what's on screen", the vision model invents plausible SaaS content (hero copy, buttons, captions) that does NOT match the actual pixels. Symptom: vision returns rich, detailed prose that contradicts `browser_snapshot`. The reliable truth source is `browser_snapshot` (the accessibility tree) + `browser_console` (DOM queries via JS). Treat vision output as decorative prose for the user, not as evidence that the page contains a given string. When verifying that a UI change landed, confirm via snapshot refs or `document.body.innerText.includes(...)`, not vision.

## SPA-in-Go pattern

When building a full single-page application embedded in a Go binary (no external files), use this pattern:

### Document template with placeholders

```go
const document = `<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{TITLE}</title>
<style>
/* ALL CSS here — dark theme, glassmorphism, responsive grid */
</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="app"></div>
<script>
/* ALL JS here — SPA router, modals, API calls */
</script>
</body>
</html>`

func page(title, body string) http.HandlerFunc {
    return func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
        if r.Method != http.MethodGet {
            http.Error(w, "method not allowed", http.StatusMethodNotAllowed)
            return
        }
        w.Header().Set("Content-Type", "text/html; charset=utf-8")
        w.Header().Set("Cache-Control", "no-store")
        s := strings.Replace(document, "{TITLE}", title, 1)
        s = strings.Replace(s, "{BODY}", body, 1)
        w.Write([]byte(s))
    }
}
```

### SPA sidebar navigation (JS)

```javascript
const state = { view: 'dashboard', models: [] };

function switchView(view) {
    state.view = view;
    render();
    // update active nav item
    document.querySelectorAll('.nav-item').forEach(el => el.classList.remove('active'));
    const active = document.querySelector(`.nav-item[onclick*="'${view}'"]`);
    if (active) active.classList.add('active');
}

function render() {
    const content = document.querySelector('.content');
    switch (state.view) {
        case 'dashboard': content.innerHTML = renderDashboard(); break;
        case 'models': content.innerHTML = renderModels(); break;
        case 'playground': content.innerHTML = renderPlayground(); break;
        case 'settings': content.innerHTML = renderSettings(); break;
    }
}
```

### Modal pattern

```javascript
function showAddModel() {
    const overlay = document.createElement('div');
    overlay.className = 'modal-overlay';
    overlay.innerHTML = '<div class="modal">' +
        '<div class="modal-header"><h2>Add Model</h2><button onclick="this.closest(\\'.modal-overlay\\').remove()">×</button></div>' +
        '<div class="modal-body">' +
        '<label>Model Name<input type="text" id="modelName"></label>' +
        '<label>Provider<select id="modelProvider">' +
        '<option>OpenAI</option><option>Anthropic</option><option>Ollama</option>' +
        '<option>Google</option><option>Mistral</option><option>Custom</option>' +
        '</select></label>' +
        '<label>Model ID<input type="text" id="modelId"></label>' +
        '<label>Base URL<input type="text" id="modelBaseUrl"></label>' +
        '<label>API Key<input type="password" id="modelApiKey"></label>' +
        '</div>' +
        '<div class="modal-footer">' +
        '<button class="btn btn-secondary" onclick="this.closest(\\'.modal-overlay\\').remove()">Cancel</button>' +
        '<button class="btn btn-primary" onclick="addModel()">Add Model</button>' +
        '</div></div>';
    document.body.appendChild(overlay);
}
```

