## Design upgrade pattern that actually works

When upgrading an inline-template UI from a v3 visual to v4:

1. Add a `:root` block of NEW design tokens at the top of the `<style>` (`--brand-500`, `--bg-2`, `--r-md`, `--e2`, etc.).
2. Add a SECOND `:root` block right after it that aliases every legacy name onto a new one (`--brand: var(--brand-600); --bg-panel: var(--bg-2);`).
3. Replace only the CSS in the `<style>` block. Leave the JS-rendered HTML untouched — its class names continue to resolve through the aliases.
4. Verify with `go clean -testcache && make test` → fresh `curl http://localhost:PORT/` → screenshot.

This works because legacy CSS never gets `var()`s of its own rewritten — the alias layer forwards them. Single CSS swap, no template rewrite, no design-system-coupled bugs in the SPA state machine.

## Pitfalls specific to this file

- The `page` const is ~1700 lines. Editors (and `patch`/`old_string` finding) slow down once the file crosses ~2000 lines; if you go beyond, split into `pageHeader`/`pageCSS`/`pageShell`/`pageFooter` constants and concatenate at `var page = pageHeader + pageCSS + pageShell + pageFooter`.
- `replace_all=true` on version tokens (`v0.3 → v0.4`) WILL splice two structurally different spans together if they happen to share the old token — bump versions by writing the surrounding 2-3 lines of context to make `old_string` unique.
- Google Fonts `<link>` is fine for localhost dev. For offline / private deployments, swap to `<style>@font-face{...}</style>` self-hosted WOFF2.
- `state.chat[]` is in-memory only — refreshing the page resets conversation. Plan persistence via `localStorage` (or skip it — many users expect playground to reset).

